<h2>Motivation</h2>
Cornell's Design Build Fly team relied almost entirely on wood for aircraft manufacturing prior to 2022. The one exception was a carbon fiber nose cone, for which the manufacturing process, materials list, and even the year it was made had been completely lost. With no institutional knowledge to build on and growing evidence that top-competing teams were winning largely due to carbon fiber's exceptional strength-to-weight ratio, I took it upon myself to start from scratch and develop an in-house composites manufacturing capability for the team. This project began in Fall 2023 and remains ongoing.

<h2>Research & Process Development</h2>
Before any fabrication could begin, I spent significant time researching carbon fiber layup processes. There was no shortage of information on the material properties and benefits of composites using outside resources, but practical manufacturing documentation for parts with the geometries we needed, hollow wings, spars, and fuselages, was almost nonexistent within the team. I built out a composites research library under the DBF 23-24's semester projects, documenting multiple manufacturing methods for parts with different structural requirements. I also collaborated with the Cornell Baja SAE composites team lead, learning their vacuum bagging layup procedure and adapting it for our purposes. Their process was designed for larger, flatter surfaces, so significant modification was needed to apply it to hollow, aerodynamically shaped aircraft components.

The core layup process involves impregnating carbon fiber sheets with epoxy resin before laying them over a mold. The assembly is built up in layers shown in figure 1, mastic tape seals the edges to a flat surface, a vacuum head is placed beneath the vacuum bag, and a pipe connects it to a vacuum pump outside. The entire layup must remain under vacuum for a minimum of 12 hours, so parts were left overnight. This was the barebones of the process and is still being iterated to this day.

