<h2>Carbon Fiber Spar</h2>
The first fabrication target was the wing spar, a hollow, square cross-section tube that serves as the primary load-bearing structure of the aircraft. Purchased spars were consistently the single largest line item in the team's annual budget, making in-house fabrication a high-value goal. To create a dissolvable mandrel, I 3D printed a solid rectangular prism using a filament that could be dissolved in a diluted chemical bath after the carbon fiber cured around it, leaving a hollow spar behind.

For the layup itself, I used two layers of carbon fiber oriented in different directions, one with fibers running horizontally and vertically, and a second layer oriented diagonally. This cross-ply approach takes advantage of the directional nature of carbon fiber's strength, producing a spar that resists both axial and shear loading more effectively than a single-orientation layup.

