The first result was far from perfect. The cross-section was not square, sections of the carbon fiber had lifted away from the mandrel, epoxy coverage was uneven, and a ridge had formed along the full length of the part where layers overlapped at the edges. An additional issue arose from the cutting process, masking tape is necessary when cutting carbon fiber to prevent the fibers from fraying, but the tape blocked epoxy from saturating the fibers in those regions, leaving dry spots visible as a lack of surface sheen. Despite these flaws, the spar was structurally promising and represented the team's first successful in-house carbon fiber fabrication. Each flaw was documented alongside proposed solutions: reducing layer overlap by precisely pre-measuring and cutting the carbon fiber, orienting the spar vertically during cure so excess epoxy pools toward the bottom where it can be trimmed, and saturating the fiber with excess resin before layup to ensure full coverage.

