<h2>Hollow Wing</h2>
Following the spar work, I turned to wing fabrication. Rather than a dissolvable 3D-printed mandrel, this iteration used a foam core with a layer of peel ply between the foam and the carbon fiber. The peel ply acts as a release layer, once the carbon fiber cures, the peel ply allows the shell to be separated from the foam, and the foam core can be cut out, yielding a hollow wing. The carbon fiber was cut much more precisely to the wing dimensions this time and wrapped more tightly around the mold, producing a result noticeably cleaner with little to no gaps in epoxy spread and a smooth useable part.

However, a critical structural issue became clear: without any internal support, the hollow shell had almost no resistance to compression. A light squeeze of the wing caused the walls to buckle inward, and it was clear the part would snap under any real aerodynamic load. The takeaway was that a hollow wing is not viable without internal structure, ribs, a spar, or a honeycomb core would be necessary to make it structurally sound. While this wing was not flight-ready, it validated the peel ply release method and marked a significant step forward in process understanding. I also used this fabrication session as a teaching opportunity, walking other team members through the layup process to begin building composites knowledge across the team.


