<h2> Hot Wire Foam Glider </h2>
For the 2024-2025 competition, I built a miniature glider out of foam using a hot wire cutter. While this part was not intended for carbon fiber layup, it was a valuable experiment in foam shaping as a potential precursor to composite manufacturing. The hot wire cutter works by passing a heated wire through foam at a controlled speed, but achieving clean, accurate airfoil shapes by hand proved to be extremely difficult. The wire speed had to be carefully regulated throughout the cut, too fast and the wire drags and distorts the shape, too slow and it melts away more material than intended. Getting consistent results required a lot of trial and error to find the right speed for each cut, and even then the surfaces required cleanup. Despite these challenges, the process gave valuable insight into what it would take to produce foam cores precise enough to serve as molds for carbon fiber layups. Foam cores are an attractive option because they are lightweight, easy to shape relative to machined molds, and can remain inside the part to add some structural support. This experiment helped establish what level of precision is achievable with a hot wire setup and what would need to improve before foam cores could be reliably used as a base for composite layups.

<h2>Carbon Fiber Fuselage</h2>
In the following year, the composites effort scaled up significantly with the fabrication of a full fuselage. Unlike the spar or wing, the fuselage had an egg-like, compound-curved geometry, a shape that presents serious challenges when trying to lay a flat, two-dimensional woven fabric over a three-dimensional surface without wrinkles, bridging, or voids. This required careful thought about how to CAD the mold for manufacturability, specifically how to split the geometry into segments that could each be laid up on a flat surface and then joined.

The fuselage was fabricated in two halves, which were then bonded together. There were initial concerns about whether the bond line would be structurally adequate, but load testing confirmed the assembled fuselage could support approximately 40 lbs, more than sufficient for our aircraft's requirements. This part was not just a prototype; it was integrated into the actual competition aircraft, making it the first structural composite airframe component the team had successfully flown.


