<h2>Future Work</h2>
Across these iterations, the most consistent lessons have been about the relationship between CAD, mold design, and manufacturability. Carbon fiber does not conform to geometry the way wet layup intuition suggests, compound curves require either gored cuts in the fabric, pre-formed pieces, or careful mold splitting. Epoxy management, fiber orientation, vacuum integrity, and release strategy all interact in ways that only become apparent through fabrication. Documenting each attempt, including failures, has been as important as the parts themselves, ensuring that the knowledge built up through this project does not disappear the way the original nose cone documentation did.

Future work focuses on a full-sized carbon fiber wing with an internal lightweight foam structure and integrated control surfaces. This would combine the hollow wing manufacturing technique with the internal support lessons learned from structural testing, and would represent a complete, flight-ready composite lifting surface, the most complex part the team has attempted to fabricate in-house.

